Subject: [PATCH] venus: back uncached host memory with cached memory when GPU writes do not arrive

0065 moved Venus's own feedback buffers to host-cached memory, but an
application can still put a CPU-read buffer in an uncached HOST_COHERENT
type. On the RTX 4060 Laptop host (Intel CPU) the guest never sees the host
GPU's writes to NVIDIA's uncached system-memory type: it keeps reading a
stale cache line.

0066's device-creation check now also tests each uncached HOST_COHERENT
type that has a host-cached counterpart (same heap, a superset of its
flags): a 4 KiB buffer in that type, written and read by the CPU, then
filled by the GPU in the same submission as the fence feedback check. A
stale read needs the CPU's line to survive until the GPU writes, so the
check re-reads the words just before submitting and runs up to three
rounds. If a fill is not visible after the fence and 20 ms, the device backs every
later allocation of that type with the cached type, unless the memory is
exported, or dedicated to a resource whose memory type bits exclude it.
Both types are HOST_COHERENT, so the application's mapping semantics are
unchanged; the GPU then writes the memory coherently with the CPU caches.

On a host where the writes arrive nothing changes. The check adds one
buffer, one 4 KiB allocation and its mapping to device creation, in the
same submissions and fence waits as 0066 (three rounds on such a host).

Evidence: docs/nvfb.md, docs/evidence/nvfb/.
Origin: vmlab-win3d nvfb worker.
---
